  "harness_epoch": 20,
  "youtube_url": "https://youtu.be/5GHINC-Wyag",
  "annotations": [
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "In an Oops All Spells deck, Thassa's Oracle doesn't need to be in hand - it will be milled into the graveyard and reanimated via Dread Return. Bottoming Unmask loses a free disruption spell that could protect the combo from countermagic or strip the opponent's interaction.",
      "action_taken": "Bottomed Unmask, keeping Thassa's Oracle in hand",
      "better_line": "Bottom Thassa's Oracle instead, keeping both Unmask (free disruption) and Thoughtseize to protect the combo line. Oracle will end up in the graveyard when the library is milled and can be reanimated with Dread Return.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 2,
      "player": "Gem25F Nature",
      "decision_index": 2,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Kozilek's Command is the only card in the opponent's hand that can directly disrupt the combo. It can exile creatures (including Thassa's Oracle or the mill creature) or exile cards from graveyards at instant speed. Tezzeret is a value engine that doesn't interact with the combo at all - by the time Tezzeret generates meaningful value, the combo should have already been attempted.",
      "action_taken": "Took Tezzeret, Cruel Captain with Thoughtseize",
      "better_line": "Take Kozilek's Command, which is the only card that can directly disrupt the Balustrade Spy/Thassa's Oracle combo by exiling creatures or graveyard cards at instant speed.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 18,
      "player": "Gem25F Nature",
      "decision_index": 10,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Kozilek's Command is the card most likely to disrupt Nature's combo. As an instant, it can exile Balustrade Spy or Undercity Informer in response to their ETB/activation, or exile critical graveyard cards like Narcomoeba. Mystic Forge is a value engine that doesn't interact with the combo at instant speed.",
      "action_taken": "Took Mystic Forge with Unmask, leaving Kozilek's Command in opponent's hand",
      "better_line": "Take Kozilek's Command with Unmask, as it's the only card in the opponent's hand that can interact with and disrupt the Balustrade Spy/Undercity Informer + Thassa's Oracle combo at instant speed",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 131,
      "player": "Gem25F Nature",
      "decision_index": 41,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Gem25F Nature is playing a Oops All Spells combo deck (no lands in library) with Balustrade Spy/Undercity Informer to mill their entire library, then win with Thassa's Oracle. They have the combo available RIGHT NOW: Exile Elvish Spirit Guide for {G}, exile another Elvish Spirit Guide for {G}, exile Simian Spirit Guide for {R}, exile another Simian Spirit Guide for {R} = {G}{G}{R}{R}. This doesn't directly cast Balustrade Spy ({3}{B}) since they need {B}. However, looking more carefully at the hand: they have exactly the mana to cast Balustrade Spy by exiling all four spirit guides (2 Elvish for {G}{G} and 2 Simian for {R}{R}) giving them 4 mana total - but they need {3}{B} specifically, requiring one black mana they don't have. So the combo isn't available this turn without black mana. BUT - the player chose 'False' (pass) and then discarded Narcomoeba at end of turn. While they can't combo this turn, they should be looking to set up for next turn. The real issue is that they're not even attempting to use spirit guides and seem confused about the exile ability being unavailable ('Since I cannot access their mana-generating ability at this precise moment'). The choices presented only show casting them as creatures, not exiling - but the player's reasoning suggests they don't understand the game state. More critically, they discard Narcomoeba - a key combo piece that enters the battlefield when milled from library. Losing a Narcomoeba reduces the devotion to blue when Thassa's Oracle resolves (they need devotion >= library size, which will be 0 after milling). They still have one Narcomoeba in hand, but discarding a combo piece is a real cost. The pass itself may be forced (no black mana available), but discarding Narcomoeba over a Spirit Guide is clearly wrong since Narcomoeba is essential to the combo.",
      "action_taken": "Passed priority without making any plays, then discarded Narcomoeba to hand size.",
      "better_line": "If forced to discard, should have discarded a Simian Spirit Guide or Elvish Spirit Guide instead of Narcomoeba. Narcomoeba provides devotion to blue on the battlefield after being milled, which is critical for Thassa's Oracle's win condition. Spirit Guides in the graveyard still get milled past (they're already in hand, not library), and having fewer spirit guides is less costly than losing a devotion source for the Oracle win.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 201,
      "player": "Gem25F Nature",
      "decision_index": 55,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Exiling Elvish Spirit Guide for {G} with no way to use the green mana this turn. There is nothing in hand that costs {G} or generic mana alone. The mana will simply be wasted, and the Spirit Guide is permanently lost from the game. This deck needs every exile-for-mana creature to eventually assemble enough mana for the Balustrade Spy combo ({3}{B}), and throwing one away for nothing significantly reduces the chance of ever comboing off.",
      "action_taken": "Exiled Elvish Spirit Guide from hand to add {G} with no way to spend it.",
      "better_line": "Pass the turn without exiling any Spirit Guides. Preserve all mana-producing creatures in hand until a black mana source (like drawing a Dark Ritual or Lotus Petal) enables the full combo in a single turn.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 255,
      "player": "Gem25F Nature",
      "decision_index": 65,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Exiled all three Spirit Guides (producing {G}{R}{R}) when none of the remaining hand cards can be cast with those colors - Balustrade Spy needs {B}, Cabal Ritual needs {B}, Cabal Therapy needs {B}, Thassa's Oracle needs {U}{U}. This wastes three irreplaceable mana sources for zero benefit, potentially destroying the ability to combo in future turns.",
      "action_taken": "Exiled Elvish Spirit Guide and both Simian Spirit Guides from hand, producing {G}{R}{R} that cannot be used to cast anything.",
      "better_line": "Pass the turn without exiling any Spirit Guides. Hold them until a black mana source (Dark Ritual, Lotus Petal, etc.) is drawn to enable the Balustrade Spy combo. The Spirit Guides could also serve as sacrifice fodder for Cabal Therapy's flashback cost.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 256,
      "player": "Gem25F Nature",
      "decision_index": 65,
    }
  ],
